{"id":"s-300-missile-system","slug":"s-300-missile-system","name":"S-300 family, NATO reporting name:, SA-10 Grumble, SA-12 Giant/Gladiator, SA-20 Gargoyle, SA-N-6 Grumble, SA-N-20 Gargoyle","aliases":["76N6","SA-10","SA-12","HQ-15","HQ-18","SA 20","SA-N-6","S-300V","S-300P","Grumble","SA-N-20","S-300PMU","Hongqi-15","Hongqi-10","Hongqi-18","SAN-6 SAM","SA-20 Triumf","SAN-6 Grumble","SA-10 Grumble","SA-20 Gargoyle","SA-N-6 Grumble","Big Bird radar","SA-12 Gladiator-Giant","SA-12 Gladiator/Giant","S-300 missile system"],"kind":"missile","category":"anti-air","subcategory":"Series of Soviet surface-to-air missile systems","description":"The S-300 is a series of long-range surface-to-air missile systems developed by the former Soviet Union. It was produced by NPO Almaz for the Soviet Air Defence Forces to defend against air raids and cruise missiles.","history":"Russian officials have stated that the system has performed well in real-world exercises. In 1991, 1992, and 1993, various versions of the S-300 destroyed ballistic missiles and other objects in exercises, with a high success rate (90% or more if 1 missile interceptor is used).\nIn 1995, it was the first system to destroy a R-17 Elbrus Scud missile in the air.\nChina is to test the S-300PMU2's effectiveness in destroying targets in real exercises. The planned targets include a UAV (4.6 kilometres (2.9 mi)), a simulated strategic bomber (186 kilometres (116 mi)), tactical missiles (range of the system to the point of interception 34 kilometres (21 mi) and a height of 17.7 kilometres (11.0 mi)), and pinpoint missiles. In April 2005, NATO held a combat exercise in France and Germany called Trial Hammer 05 to practice Suppression of Enemy Air Defenses missions. The Slovak Air Force brought an S-300PMU along, providing an opportunity for NATO to become familiar with the system.\nIsrael's purchase of F-35 Lightning II fighters was allegedly intended in part to nullify the threat of S-300 missiles that were, at the time the fighters were initially sought, part of a potential arms sale to Iran. The fighter jets were eventually deployed by Israel in late October 2024 and disabled Iran's last three S-300 batteries in the first wave in a three-waved airstrike, thereby severely compromising Iran's air defenses.\nIn 2010, Russia announced that its military had deployed the S-300 systems in breakaway Abkhazia in 2008, leading to condemnation from the government of Georgia.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer (Almaz-Antey)","value":"NPO Almaz (lead designer)"},{"label":"Designer (Almaz-Antey)","value":"NIIP (radars)"},{"label":"Designer (Almaz-Antey)","value":"MKB Fakel (missile designer for S-300P series)"},{"label":"Designer (Almaz-Antey)","value":"NPO Novator (missile designer for S-300V series)"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1967–2005"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"MZiK"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1975–2011 (for PS and PM)"},{"label":"Unit Cost (Export cost)","value":"US$120–150 million (FY 2010) for a battery"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Soviet Union"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1978–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Designer":"NPO Almaz (lead designer)","Designed":"1967–2005","Manufacturer":"MZiK","Produced":"1975–2011 (for PS and PM)","Unit Cost":"US$120–150 million (FY 2010) for a battery","Origin":"Soviet Union","In Service":"1978–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/S-300_-_2009_Moscow_Victory_Day_Parade_%282%29.jpg","width":2250,"height":1418,"license":"See Wikimedia Commons"},"thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/S-300_-_2009_Moscow_Victory_Day_Parade_%282%29.jpg/330px-S-300_-_2009_Moscow_Victory_Day_Parade_%282%29.jpg"},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Caracas%2C_Canciller_Ricardo_Pati%C3%B1o_particip%C3%B3_en_los_actos_de_conmemoraci%C3%B3n_de_la_muerte_de_Hugo_Ch%C3%A1vez_%2812960